//! Architecture contract and local CI tools.

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const GET_ARCHITECTURE_CONTRACT: &str = "Read the local target-architecture contract at .weavatrix/architecture.json. state CONFIGURED means that file is loaded, and the answer includes the contract plus a bounded observed summary. No file yields the not-configured guidance and a starter. action preview with no file returns state PREVIEW, the derived starter, and write NONE. action approve is an error: this MCP server never writes the contract. candidate_contract, baseline_mode, and confirm_token are accepted only as read-only preview inputs. The declared style is not evidence of the observed architecture. Call architecture_inventory for hypotheses and verify_architecture for the verdict.";

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const ARCHITECTURE_INVENTORY: &str = "Infer evidence-bounded architecture hypotheses from observed code and typed dependencies: modular source, onion, ports-and-adapters, layered, and deployment topology. The declared target style is not evidence. Omit detail for a bounded summary: complete graph totals, representative paths, dominant coupling, and cycle candidates. Passing max_results or edge_cursor without detail forces detail full. summary together with max_results or edge_cursor is an error: summary does not page edges; use detail=full. Full mode pages edges (default 200, maximum 500). When the max_results key is present, the page also caps components, packages, cycles, workspaces, and runners, and each edge keeps one evidence file pair. analysis_id and status are computed on the full projection before that slimming, so the cap does not change the verdict. status is INCOMPLETE on a diagnostic, incomplete capture, a truncated edge page, more than 50 cyclic SCCs, or a condensation past its component, edge, or generation cap. Hypotheses are not paged. token_budget can still trim a summary and then mark it INCOMPLETE. edge_cursor continues a full edge page and is bound to the captured graph.";

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const CI_RESTRICTIONS: &str = "Local GitHub Actions jobs, literal check invocations, configured restrictions, and the enforcement this tree does not show. Presence of a workflow is not a run and not a remote required check. Dynamic commands and mutually exclusive configuration unions stay unknown. status stays INCOMPLETE while unresolved entries remain. Omit max_results for the full local report, with the restrictions array capped at 100 (maximum 500). Pass max_results to cap restrictions and to slim workflow and config side channels: a job's steps field becomes a count. scope and scenario select which files and which changed-path case are considered. token_budget trims the tail. Use explain_restriction with an id from a restrictions row. This is not ci_protection.";

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const CI_PROTECTION: &str = "Candidate local checks and declared architecture-rule bindings for an explicit file list. files is required. Pass the paths you care about. The tool does not read the git diff and does not run workflows. Membership and component ids in the answer are path labels. A single file can return INCOMPLETE with an explicit reason when the binding cannot be proved. change_impact, graph_diff, verified_change, and prepare_change do not include this block; call this tool when you want it.";

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const EXPLAIN_RESTRICTION: &str = "Explain one local restriction: its invocation, when it applies, and the evidence it does not have. id is required and comes from a ci_restrictions restrictions row. A known id returns status FOUND and the restriction. An unknown id such as a made-up token returns status NOT_FOUND. status INCOMPLETE means the restriction list itself was truncated past 500, so the id might exist on a later page. scenario is forwarded to the same report. Local workflow presence is not execution and not remote merge enforcement.";

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const VERIFY_ARCHITECTURE: &str = "Verify graph dependencies against the active .weavatrix/architecture.json. No contract returns state NOT_CONFIGURED, enforceable false, empty new, and a starter, with write NONE. A loaded contract returns enforceable true. state BLOCKED means new is non-empty. state PASS means new is empty. Read new[].fingerprint for each blocking violation. That fingerprint is the argument to explain_architecture_violation and propose_architecture_exception. warnings are warn-severity rules and do not block. existing are baselined fingerprints, excepted are accepted exceptions, and fixed are baseline fingerprints no longer present. Each blocking row carries rule.id, rule.action, and rule.severity. Unknown rule actions fail closed. This does not edit the contract. A BLOCKED result on the current tree is the verdict.";

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const VERIFY_CAPABILITIES: &str = "Resolve declared capabilities in .weavatrix/architecture.json against the endpoints this revision exposes, in both directions. No contract returns state NOT_CONFIGURED. A contract with no capabilities section returns state NOT_DECLARED, enforceable false, and a starter. Otherwise state BLOCKED or PASS reports served, drifted, and orphaned claims. unmapped is trimmed by max_results, and unmapped_total is the full count. include_tests and include_classified add those endpoints. This checks declarations against static endpoints. It does not deploy a service.";

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const EXPLAIN_ARCHITECTURE_VIOLATION: &str = "Explain one active contract violation. fingerprint is required and comes from verify_architecture new (or another active row). A fingerprint that is not an active violation, including a placeholder such as none, returns state NOT_FOUND and does not invent a violation. A hit returns state EXPLAINED, the fingerprint, and a brief violation. No contract returns the not-configured response. Nothing is written.";

pub(in crate::operations::catalog) const PROPOSE_ARCHITECTURE_EXCEPTION: &str = "Return a reviewable exception proposal and write nothing. fingerprint and reason are required. fingerprint comes from an active verify_architecture violation. An unknown fingerprint returns state NOT_FOUND. A hit returns state PROPOSAL_ONLY, write NONE, and a proposal that echoes fingerprint, reason, the optional expires value, and a brief violation. Save and review any exception yourself in .weavatrix/architecture.json. This tool does not approve it.";
